<Base
  title="About · Andreas Jackson"
  description="About Andreas (AJ) Jackson: raised on a farm, studied business at Berkeley Haas, consulted for the Gates Foundation and IKEA, interned in London and Tokyo, now an analyst at EY building things with AI."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose-wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Hi, I'm AJ.</h1>
      <p>
        I grew up on a farm raising pigs, cows, and chickens. The first pig I
        raised and sold, I cried over. Then my dad told me the money was mine to
        put into any stock I wanted, and because I was a kid and Walmart was the
        store I knew, I bought Walmart. That was the first time it occurred to
        me that money could do something other than sit in a drawer, and it's
        more or less the reason I ended up in business school.
      </p>
      <p>
        The school was UC Berkeley's Haas School of Business, class of 2025. I
        got into the Global Management Program, which took me to London for a
        semester and permanently changed the altitude I think at. I like
        problems that make more sense when you look at them globally, I like
        learning how other places actually work, and I like traveling somewhere
        new and figuring out how to squeeze the most out of every dollar while
        I'm there. That last habit turns out to be the same instinct I bring to
        work.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also took data science courses at Berkeley, mostly out of curiosity,
        and found the part I enjoyed most was building something from nothing
        and watching it solve a problem I actually had. That's still the part I
        enjoy most.
      </p>

      <h2>Consulting, and what stuck</h2>
      <p>
        I spent three years at Berkeley ABA Consulting, working my way from
        analyst to project manager to senior advisor. The client work included
        IKEA, T-Mobile, and the Gates Foundation. For two semesters I was the
        project manager on the nonprofit team, running engagements for The
        Assistance Fund and the Gates Foundation.
      </p>
      <p>
        That nonprofit work is the piece I'd point to if you asked what I want
        my career to add up to. I'd like the long arc of it to leave things
        better than I found them, and the way I think I can contribute is by
        building useful things and being honest about whether they work.
      </p>
      <p>
        Alongside that I did two international internships: one in London, and
        one with a Tokyo-based company that sent me to CES. Both were small
        enough that the work was real, and both are why ambiguity doesn't
        bother me much.
      </p>

      <h2>Now</h2>
      <p>
        I'm an analyst at EY in San Francisco, in the financial services
        assurance practice, working across banking engagements.
      </p>
      <p>
        In my free time I build. Almost all of it now runs through AI tools,
        Claude Code in particular: this website,
        <a href="/projects/nottomorrow">NotTomorrow</a>, a
        <a href="/projects/deadlock-counter-buy">recommendation engine</a> for a
        game I play, and
        <a href="/projects/scaffolding-vs-scale">a research study</a> on how AI
        models behave on real occupational work. That last one taught me more
        about research than any coursework did, mostly because it went wrong in
        an interesting way.
      </p>

      <h2>What I'm looking for</h2>
      <p>
        A full-time role where analytical rigor and shipping speed both matter:
        AI implementation and applied AI, product management, data and
        analytics, strategy and operations, or strategic finance. Anywhere in
        the Bay Area works for me, San Francisco through the Peninsula and
        South Bay, and I'm also open to Santa Barbara or remote.
      </p>

      <h2>Off the clock</h2>
      <p>
        I'm competitive, and most of what I do for fun reflects that. Basketball
        and soccer, skiing, chess, and training seriously enough that fitness
        shapes how I structure a week. Video games too, which is how the
        recommendation engine on this site started life.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rest is reading and travel. A weekly economics newsletter, the
        semiconductor and data-center industry (yes, I've read Chip War), and
        planning the next trip somewhere I haven't been.
      </p>

